On Fri, 18 Aug 2006, azgor wrote:
>
> [1]: Characters with very low dexterity should have intrinsic fumbling.
> --Grunt

   Reasoning okay; playability awful. They don't need more trouble. :(


> [2]: Vorpal Blade against jabberwocks should produce the yafm: "Thou
> hast slain the Jabberwock!" as opposed to the normal death message.
> --Grunt

   The quotation is really "And hast thou slain the Jabberwock?", a
question, which is less conducive to YAFMs.
   Extra-hard-to-get YAFM: If you try to sacrifice a jabberwock that
wasn't killed by you (e.g., by a trap, pet, or conflict), your god
refuses the sacrifice with the above skeptical remark. ;)


> [3]: If you grease your gauntlets, slippery finger effects should take
> place. Further, greased weapons should slip from your hands (probably
> by making your hands greasy). --Grunt

   Greased gauntlets maybe. Greased weapons no. Even someone with Int 3
is smart enough not to grease the part you hold. And maybe greased gloves
are only greased on the backs; but I guess if the grease prevents rusting
when the gloves are immersed in water, it must be all over, even on the
palms.


> [6]: Pets should be much less likely than other monsters to "back away
> skeptically" when you try to #untrap them, and peaceful monsters less
> likely than hostile ones. --PsyMar

   Actually, I'd expect hostile monsters to snap at you and/or try to
attack you, rather than doing anything as peaceful as backing away.
The rest of this YANI is another of those "so obvious I thought it was
already implemented" ideas.


> [8]: Dipping fruit into a potion of booze should make foo-foo drinks;
> ie. a banana + booze = a banana daiquiri.  etc. --workimer

   ITYM "frou-frou".  Too much work for no gameplay effect.


> [9]: Monsters that cast spells should have, say 50% change of carrying
> the spellbooks for the spells they know. --ijon

   It'd be way too easy to get spellbooks then.


> [12]: Potion of ipecac.  Stuns you for a few turns and reduces your
> hunger status to just above Weak, but cures (or has a chance of curing)
> food poisoning. --cjeris

   Angband's salt water does that. Maybe the water on Medusa's level should
be salty. Throwing salt water on puddings, slimes, and gelatinous cubes
should hurt them. (Does salt water have an effect on zombies, or is that
just undissolved salt?) But those effects alone don't justify a new potion
type, to me. What else could salt water do?


> [14]: Fireproof candles should not be able to be lit. --Grunt

   Isn't this a known issue? ISTR fireproof candles being filed as a bug;
maybe they won't be in the game at all in the next release.


> [17]: Candy bars become a different candy based on the holiday.
> Christmas = Candy cane; easter = marshmallow chicks; Valentine's day =
> candy heart or box of chocolates; etc. --aristotle73

   Wouldn't make sense internationally.  ("Your boots are filled with
fruit and nuts! You have trouble walking.")


> [18]: Polymorphing a crocodile corpse, instead of creating fireproof
> low boots, should create fireproof water walking boots. Crocs arent so
> common as to make this unbalancing. --jmr

   But you only need one croc. And they're way more common if you 
reverse-genocide them for the boots. Bad idea.


> [19]: Wands of cold can be used to write on water. --aristotle73

   Interesting...

